## Video Episodes
- ['A Cursed Era': Anand Giridharadas on When Jordan Neely and Daniel Penny Met on a NYC Subway](https://www.ms.now/video-podcast/a-cursed-era-anand-giridharadas-on-when-jordan-neely-and-daniel-penny-met-on-a-nyc-subway-2517090883853): In 2023, a former Marine strangled a homeless Michael Jackson impersonator to death on a New York City subway\. The story became national news, as Americans chose sides, either demanding justice for another Black man’s death at the hands of a white man or defending the actions of a former soldier who said he believed he was protecting the car’s passengers\. Anand Giridharadas chronicled this deadly chapter in American history and its aftermath in his latest book Man in the Mirror\. He dissects the divisions that have been spreading in the country in this – as he calls it – age of estrangement\. "In a case about dehumanization,” he says\. “I wanted to humanize everybody\. ”
- [Nicolle and Aaron Sorkin: This is Trump’s “ultimate mind f\*\*”](https://www.ms.now/video-podcast/nicolle-and-aaron-sorkin-this-is-trump-s-ultimate-mind-f-2516825155672): Aaron Sorkin wasn’t planning to make another movie about Facebook — until he met a whistleblower whose story he felt compelled to tell\. In an expansive conversation with Nicolle Wallace, the Oscar\- and Emmy\-winning writer and director discusses his new film, The Social Reckoning, and the extraordinary power Mark Zuckerberg and Facebook wield over nearly 3 billion people — and the costly decisions that have shaped the platform’s impact on our lives, our politics and our democracy\.
A follow\-up to 2010’s The Social Network, Sorkin’s new film examines Facebook in the age of Trump, telling the story of whistleblower Frances Haugen’s decision to expose internal documents detailing misinformation and potential harms, while exploring an older Zuckerberg confronting the consequences of the technology he created\. Sorkin explains why he chose to release the film ahead of the midterm elections and why he believes there is still room for optimism — if we are willing to act\.

Wallace and Sorkin explore Zuckerberg’s immense influence, the decisions surrounding Facebook on January 6, the people inside the company who knew what was happening and remained silent, and why Wallace sees Zuckerberg as “bubble wrapped from reality\.” They discuss the complicated empathy the film invites audiences to feel for Zuckerberg — and how that empathy can coexist with accountability for decisions that affect billions\. Sorkin also shares the story of Zuckerberg’s former consigliere and their falling out, his reaction to Jeremy Strong’s portrayal of Zuckerberg \(whom Sorkin calls “the best American actor under 50 years old”\), and Facebook’s efforts to obtain the film’s script before its release\. He reveals how the filmmakers prepared for potential legal challenges, including the threat of a lawsuit\.

The conversation also examines Trump’s relationship with Zuckerberg, the former president’s attacks on the media despite his apparent appetite for attention from credible news organizations, and the essential role investigative journalism plays in holding powerful people and institutions accountable\. Wallace and Sorkin discuss why Congress and the law continue to lag behind rapidly advancing technology, the consequences of leaving that power unchecked, and why the stakes for democracy could hardly be higher\.

And in a moment that connects Sorkin’s celebrated work across generations, the conversation turns to A Few Good Men, Hegseth’s “illegal order” and the enduring importance of questioning authority\. Wallace, who was inspired by Allison Janney’s portrayal of White House Press Secretary C\.J\. Cregg on The West Wing to imagine a future for herself in the White House, joins Sorkin in reflecting on the responsibilities of the press, the dangers of unchecked power and the choices that define our political moment\.

From Facebook’s influence over billions of lives to Trump, whistleblowers, investigative journalism and the future of American democracy, Sorkin’s message is clear: We need to take democracy more seriously\.
- [Michael Steele: The Republican Party Needs an ENEMA](https://www.ms.now/video-podcast/michael-steele-the-republican-party-needs-an-enema-2516600387720): Michael Steele remains a Republican, but the party as it stands today is unrecognizable to him\. “For the sake of power, we bought the idea of the South will rise again,” he says\. “We should've killed it in its infancy and \[been\] done with it because it gave rise to what happened on January 6th\.” The first Black chairman of GOPAC and the RNC joins the groupchat to offer his take on big money \(and dark money\) in politics, to reflect on how his faith guides his politics, and to explain why he’s stuck with the Republicans in spite of their about\-face with regard to individual liberties\. “ The easiest thing to do is walk away,” says Steele\. “ I have to believe in your rights as a citizen under the flag and the Constitution to be on this soil, to live on this soil, to flourish on this soil – however we find you on this soil\.”
